double get total => expenses.fold(0, (sum, e) => sum + e.amount);
The getter part: double get total =>
get makes total a getter. 
A getter looks like a field when you use it,
but it actually runs code every time it is read.
You write state.total, with no parentheses, exactly as if it were a stored value.



The reason to calculate the total instead of storing it is that a stored total 
could get out of sync with the list 
(for example, if you remove an expense but forget to update the total).
A getter is always recalculated from the list, so it is always correct.
